abstract: >-
  A zero-Mathlib Lean 4.35.0 formalization (checked by six proof kernels, with zero
  native_decide and no sorry outside the statement-only comparator stub), companion
  Rust/C11/Python verification engines, and an AMS-LaTeX preprint. Lean proves: (1) lifting
  of independent sets from two quotient graphs to C_n^4 for the subgroups
  H_BPZ = <(1,0,0,2),(0,1,2,0)> and H* = <(1,0,0,2),(0,1,0,4)>, double-counting ceilings for
  these quotients, and two families of coset-union codes for n = 4m+1 and n = 8k+1 that meet
  them; many of these sizes were published by Baumert et al. (1971) and Codenotti, Gerace and
  Resta (2003); (2) four lower bounds on the Shannon capacity of odd cycles:
  Theta(C_25) >= 12.344196091426841 and Theta(C_29) >= 14.319949949077991, which exceed the
  largest published values found (Codenotti-Gerace-Resta 2003, Table 1), and
  Theta(C_27) >= 13.319390984674515 and Theta(C_31) >= 15.308776193986504, which are below
  the values in that table; (3) a two-alphabet form of the BPZ combining rule (Tandon,
  arXiv:2608.30273, Proposition 1), admissibility of five BPZ tables, a weighted
  clique-cover upper bound, and alpha(G_7^d) <= 3^d for a 7-label graph; and (4) integer
  forms of Haemers's rank bound, Lovasz's pentagon bound and Haemers's 1979 Schlafli-graph
  example. Theta is not defined in Lean; capacity bounds follow from integer statements.
  No published open problem is resolved, and exact Theta(C_n) for odd n >= 7 remains open.
